        "id": 264088,
        "name": "",
        "username": "LonelyMan",
        "avatar_template": "/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/l/f4b2a3/{size}.png",
        "created_at": "2025-10-21T13:19:34.954Z",
        "cooked": "<p>Fall of the Prime Wardens has a lot in common with Vengeance; I wish it had a set of mini-villain decks to go with it.  Bugbear definitely counts, apart from being a Nightmist nemesis; other than that, I’d likely do a Balerion deck, a Galactra deck, a Blood Countess deck that’s Fanatic’s enemy (and has Apostate as a target within it), and then one more for Kismet or FILTER or something.</p>\n<aside class=\"quote no-group quote-modified\" data-username=\"Trajector\" data-post=\"15\" data-topic=\"19108\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/trajector/48/1914_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"> Trajector:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>As it happens, before I listened I was thinking about Haka. Haka is interesting because his personality and history really doesn’t come through in his deck at all. He’s supposed to be a wise, supportive teacher - but his deck makes him into more of a solo bruiser.</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>I strongly theorize, and assume C&amp;A would never admit they used to be as “problematic” as that…when they first designed Haka, as the fifth character after Legacy, Wraith, Cold-Guns AZ, and the Shrieker (who likely morphed gradually and drastically into Tachyon), they probably envisioned him as a very Hulk-like character who was also a  massive Maori stereotype, including the idea (whether or not it’s true) of the Maori being former cannibals.  Savage Mana really really reads like Haka is swallowing people whole and then puking them back up; they probably abandoned that long before making the original art with Hippo, but I really suspect this was the original idea from 2009.  Remember the two pre-Mark Ruffalo Hulk movies; those would have inspired Haka the same way Iron Man inspired Bunker.</p>\n<p>This story works better with my concept of Haka being ultra-human rather than superhuman; his blood isn’t super-empowering to the CoB, it’s just human blood, but he has gallons more of it and can spare a good ten percent more of the volume before passing out or worse.  Filter sees him as a human, and cuts a deal with him to keep Tempest under control.  It works so much better if he’s immortal and developed far beyond normal human limits, but not actually super-strong in the way the Legacies are.</p>\n<p>I love Adam in this episode, cracking up at Christopher’s plural of “flesh child” and then absolutely noping out of the Autumn of the PW letter.</p>",
